is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR BGP peering configuration on router1 (bgpd process).

Mechanism: Because router1 lacks a BGP neighbor configuration toward router2, no TCP session is formed on the interconnect 193.10.11.0/24. Router2 continuously initiates BGP (state Active) but router1 never responds as a BGP speaker. With no eBGP session, neither side installs the remote LAN prefix, so pc1’s packets for 200.1.1.0/24 are not routed off-net, causing pc1→pc2 pings to fail while local gateway reachability (195.11.14.1) still works.

Localization: On router1, interfaces are up (eth0 193.10.11.1/24, eth1 195.11.14.1/24). FRR is running but “show ip bgp summary” reports “No BGP neighbors found,” and the running-config shows router bgp 1 with only “network 195.11.14.0/24” and no neighbor statements. Router1’s routing table contains only connected routes. On router2, the eBGP neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1 is configured; “show ip bgp summary” shows the neighbor stuck in Active, confirming one-sided peering attempts.

Root cause: The BGP neighbor toward router2 is missing on router1 (likely an inadvertent configuration removal or not saved/applied).

Corrective fix: On router1, configure the eBGP neighbor toward router2 and save:
- router bgp 1
- neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2
- address-family ipv4 unicast
  (optional) neighbor 193.10.11.2 activate
- end; write memory
After this, BGP should establish, routes 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 will exchange, and pc1↔pc2 connectivity will be restored.
=== END ===